    "## Project Goal:\n",
    "\n",
    "This project aims to address the following question: Which measure has a stronger correlation with the progression of the clinical severity score for patients with FSHD, dorsiflexion strength progression or stir quant progression? \n",
    "This question matters in evaluating treatments in a clinical-trial setting as it aims to find the more influential clinical evaluation measure for progression of FSHD. By finding a better measure resources can be spent on enhancing that measure which can lead to more knowledge about the disease increasing the likelihood of developing a treatment.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "91408b75-185a-4d66-9ce7-7c7aabbde5e1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Disease Background: \n",
    "- ***Please provide citations and/or hyperlinks to where you obtained information (including AI) for each of the bullet points.***\n",
    "\n",
    "* Prevalence & incidence: The prevalence rate of FSHD is 1 case per 8,000. The incidence, based on population studies in the Netherlands, is 0.3/1000,0000 persons-years, or 52 diagnosed people in the Netherlands. However, more cases remain misdiagnosed or undiagnosed. Source 1: https://www.mda.org/disease/facioscapulohumeral-muscular-dystrophy\n",
    "Source 2: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4166358/\n",
    "* Symptoms: Facial weakness is often the earliest sign of FSHD, making it hard to use a straw or smile. Other hallmark symptoms include shoulder weakness, causing scapular winging, and lower leg weakness, causing a foot drop which increases the risk of tripping and difficulty walking. Additionally, chronic pain is common in FSHD patients due to strain related to posture and weakness. Source: https://www.mda.org/disease/facioscapulohumeral-muscular-dystrophy/signs-and-symptoms\n",
    "* Diagnosis: Diagnosis of FSHD is usually suspected based on a pattern of muscle weakness, typically in the face shoulder blades and upper arms, often with asymmetric involvement. The most reliable way to confirm a diagnosis is through genetic testing. A contraction of the D4Z4 region on chromosome 4 identifies this disease. In some cases where a diagnosis is unclear, blood tests that measure creatine kinase, EMGs, or muscle biopsies can also be used. Source: https://www.mda.org/disease/facioscapulohumeral-muscular-dystrophy/diagnosis\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "* Standard of care treatments (& reimbursement): Patients suffering from FSHD often begin occupational and/or physical therapy in order to adapt performance daily tasks manage pain and other symptoms. Occupational therapists often observe the patient in their home environment in order to suggest ways of improving efficiency and comfort in doing tasks or the use of assistive technology such as wheelchairs. Physical therapists work with patients to perform exercises to rehabilitate their muscles. Physical therapy is often prescribed by primary care doctors when a patient's FSHD symptoms progress more quickly than usual, in order to adapt to their worsened muscle strength. Further, scapular fixation surgery is a common procedure that attaches the scapula to the ribcage in order to stabilize the shoulder. The surgery is performed on patients that no longer can raise their arm above their shoulder, which greatly hinders daily activities. Source: https://www.fshdsociety.org/living-with-fshd/managing-fshd/clinical-care/\n",
    "\n",
    "* Disease progression & prognosis: FSHD is a slow progressing disease that gradually deteriorates the muscle fibers in the body, causing muscle weakness. Per the name of the disease, the face, shoulder, and upper arm muscle regions often degenerate the most, although all muscles of the body can be affected. Disease progression rate and severity varies by individual. In rare cases, patients with FSHD develop respitory, hearing, or vissoin issues, especially when the disease is early-onset and begins in childhood. Source: https://www.mda.org/disease/facioscapulohumeral-muscular-dystrophy\n",
    "\n",
    "* Biological mechanisms (anatomy, organ physiology, cell & molecular physiology): FSHD is an epigenetic condition due to the 4q35 region in chromosome 4. This region is a modulator of DUX4 activity. Although the DUX4 gene is important for embryological development, in healthy adults the gene shuts down due to D4Z4 array, which prevents DUX4 from being transcribed any further. A healthy D4Z4 array typically has more than 10 repeats, causing it to be longer and more compact. However, in FSHD patients, the D4Z4 array has less than 10 repeats, causing it to be less compact. In FSHD1, the D4Z4 chromosomal tandem repeats are deleted, causing the continued transcription of the DUX4 gene and therefor the secretion of the DUX4 protein. In FSHD2, the D4Z4 chromatin has repeats but is not contracted due to a dysfunctional SMCHD1 (structural maintenance of chromosomes flexible hinge domain containing 1) protein, also causing the transcription and secretion of DUX4. The over-expression of DUX4 leads to progressive muscle degeneration due to muscle cell apoptosis or impairment of muscle differentiation. This eventually leads to muscle fiber loss and fatty infiltration. The expression of DUX4 is not constant in all muscles in the body, causing some tissues to deteriorate more quickly than others. Sources: class lecture, https://www.fshdsociety.org/living-with-fshd/understanding-fshd/genetic-cause/\n",
    "\n",
    "* Clinical Trials/next-gen therapies: Research on molecular, biological, and gene therapies are being conducted to slow or stop FSHD progression. The main goal of the drugs are to restore immune balance, support muscles, or block DUX4 expression. Although a majority if the drugs are still in the research phase, three are in clinical phase 1 (Restem-L / ULSC, Apitegromab (SRK-015), and Clenbuterol), three are in phase 2 (Satralizumab, ARO-DUX4 / SRP-1001, and EPI-321), and one is in phase 3 (Del-brax / AOC 1020). While most therapies aim to block the DUX4 gene, research is also being conducted on supplement development and physical therapy practices. Source: https://www.fshdsociety.org/research-and-clinical-care/research-and-clinical-trials/drug-development-pipeline/"
   ]
  },
