   "source": "Quantitative Short-TI Inversion Recovery (STIR Quant) was used as [measure]([https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7190715/](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7190715/)) of inflammation and fluid buildup at the mid-point of the tibialis anterior. Similarly to fat fraction percentage, SITR Quant is a dimensionless quantitative anatomical measure that can demonstrate the beginnings of muscular dystrophy in FSHD patients. In addition to STIR Quant, dorsiflexion strength (measured in Newtons) was used in the following analysis as a functional measure of the patients' FSHD. Finally, the Clinical Severity Score on a scale of 0-10 was used as a clinical measure to compare with STIR Quant and dorsiflexion strength. All three measures were interpolated to the lowest period of time from baseline at T1 = 11.15 months and T2 =  22.13 months from the baseline measurement. If a patient did not have a T2 data point, they were removed from the T2 analysis. The following code imports and pulls the patient data from the CSV file, calculates the interpolations, and provides an example plot of the interpolation of four patients.",
   "id": "bf18de12dfa62329"
