    "## Conclusions:\n",
    "\n",
    "Although our linear regression lines showed a slight positive correlation for dorsiflexion and a small negative correlation for STIR quant, our data analysis revealed no correlation between clinical severity percent change and STIR quant or dorsiflexion change. Additional evidence such as a larger sample size or more consistent data would most likely allow us to form a more concrete conclusion. If a concrete conclusion could be made, the conclusion could be used to focus resources on a clinical trial of the better biomarker. This could potentially save resources which could be allocated to better understand FSHD.  However, if one biomarker was chosen over another it would look different in each patient and wouldn’t give the full picture of each patient’s experience. There is a real risk of overlooking reduced function in many other areas of a patient’s life. Therefore, for diseases like FSHD a variety of biomarkers should be used to describe a patient’s loss of function."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f023b735-6efb-43ed-a03d-eb4a9cdb734e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Limitations and Future Work:\n",
    "\n",
    "When conducting our data analysis we took an average of each data set for the right and left leg since patient functionality is often dictated by an average of the left and right legs. However, this means we may have overlooked the effects of uneven functionality or other possible losses in functionality. The small sample size makes it hard to make concrete conclusions because of the large variance in the data. If given more time we would want to include more patients in the study. We would also analyze over a longer time so the progression of the disease is more concrete, and we could potentially get a relevant conclusion. Finally, if we analyzed the more muscle groups, we would have a better idea of the functional effects of FSHD."
   ]
  },
